     '| **Spectral / operator** (Connes’ adele class space, Hilbert–Pólya, Berry–Keating, Dirac / Weitzenböck / Bochner–Kodaira–Nakano) | the constraint (scaling action; zeros as absorption spectrum); a Weitzenböck split of the Weil form into a boundary block and a relative curvature bound | slots 1, 4; condition (2) | Weitzenböck split 3.52 [Restatement]; BKN curvature k/y² 3.48 [Theorem] | the split is sharp but circular; the curvature is Γ data, identical for Davenport–Heilbronn; no first-order operator exists (the archimedean symbol is log-order) |',
     '| **Cohomological / Hodge index** (Deninger, the 𝔽_q model, Spec ℤ × Spec ℤ) | the only model where all four conditions hold: Weil form = Gram matrix of the Frobenius orbit, positivity = Hodge index / Rosati | conditions (1)–(4) as template; slot 2 | 𝔽_q: Theorem (all four pass) | no such cohomology over Spec ℤ is constructed; Deninger’s is conjectural |',
     '| **Automorphic / Hecke** (Ramanujan–Petersson, Petersson inner product, Jacobi U_l / V_l, Eichler–Shimura F + V, theta lifts, 691) | positive Hilbert spaces with Hecke action; V_p*V_p = p^{−(k−2)}(p^{k−2}+α)(p^{k−2}+β); the 691 congruence module | conditions (1), (2); slot 2 candidate | V*V edge positivity 3.48 [Theorem]; T_ℤ ≅ {(a,b): a ≡ b mod 691} 3.28 [Theorem] | positivity excludes only the edge (Satake offset δ ≥ ½); F and V change level while positivity lives at one level; DH is invisible on the Jacobi side (fails (4)); 691 is an integral degeneracy with no sign |',
     '| **Arithmetic / Iwasawa / CM** (imaginary quadratic fields, the discriminant, class numbers, Mazur–Wiles) | exact boundary data and global counts: h⁺ vs h sees sign in one ℤ/2 at the real places; one B₁₂ governs four boundary objects; U_l, V_l, T_p preserve the fundamental discriminant | slot 1; condition (3) template (built from Euler data) | narrow/wide class group 3.08 [Theorem]; discriminant-family invariance 3.20 [Theorem] | finite-place data are sign-blind (genus theory is phase data); Weil positivity lacks Mazur–Wiles’ exact global count (Analogy 2.48) |',
     '| **Statistical mechanics / thermodynamics** (primon gas, Lee–Yang / Newman Φ, de Bruijn–Newman, contact / Legendre) | the former rank-1 entry (pin removed in v1.16): a ferromagnetic / Lee–Yang reason for the global cancellation; Fock space of unique factorization | condition (1); slot 2 candidate | primon Fock space 3.4 [Theorem]; former rank-1 Φ entry [Open, priority 0.380, now rank 289] | Φ > 0 holds for Davenport–Heilbronn too (fails (4)); per-prime ferromagnetic signs refuted; Newman class 𝒫 fails; real-field thermodynamics is RH-blind; Lee–Yang Φ test done: single-spin = RH (Restatement 3.4), many-spin adds nothing, GHS/moments blind (3.1/3.4), no λ-invariant local condition suffices (Theorem 3.7); ferromagnetic coupling heals DH like positive de Bruijn time (3.3). |',
     '| **String / VOA no-ghost** (Goddard–Thorn, Borcherds, the V♮ orbifold) | the shape of the missing piece: constraint + Lorentzian II₁,₁ + positive transverse factor ⇒ no ghosts | slot 1 (exists), slot 2 (missing); conditions (2), (4) via DDF and c = 26 | RH ⇔ no-ghost 3.40 [Restatement]; II₁,₁ ↔ pole pair 3.20 [Analogy] | no DDF analogue (Open 2.28), no c = 26 analogue (Open 1.76); no-ghost transports positivity, it does not create it |',
     '| **Classical mechanics / geometry** (Jacobi constant, Lagrange points, Kepler, light cone / Hodge-index skeleton) | dictionaries: elliptic / parabolic / hyperbolic ⇔ a² vs 4q; the first-class constraint surface is coisotropic and its reduction is the Goddard–Thorn quotient | slot 1 frame; interpretation of conditions (2), (4) | coisotropic reduction 3.0 [Theorem]; zero velocity ↔ null states 2.2 [Analogy] | analogies only; ghost-blind (they see a ghost only when it arrives) |',
     '| **Function fields / Fermat curves** (Weil 1949 Jacobi sums, Hasse–Davenport, Bombieri–Stepanov, FLT / R = T) | RH over 𝔽_q is literal: &#124;J&#124; = √q from &#124;g&#124;² = q, one root per Hecke-character L-polynomial; Davenport–Heilbronn-type fakes over 𝔽_q keep the functional equation and positive integral point counts yet fail RH | condition (4) located: the surface (Hodge index on C×C), not counting positivity | Numerical 3.5; Theorem 3.4 | over ℚ the Fermat L-functions are Hecke L-functions whose RH is as open as ζ’s; R = T forces automorphy, which ζ already has |',
     '',
     '## Latest results (v1.15)',
     '',
     '> The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved. The headline figure of v1.15 is the refined missing piece; below it are the no-ghost figures. Labels and scores as in the entries; “0–4” scores measure closeness to a literal bridge (0 decoration, 2 structural, 3 literal in a model, 4 literal and load-bearing). The v1.14 and earlier galleries follow below.',
     '',
     '![The refined missing piece: a no-ghost theorem for the scaling reduction](figures/hero_v115/missing_piece_no_ghost.png)', '', '*The refined missing piece. Top: Borcherds’ no-ghost mechanism ingredient by ingredient against ζ’s scaling-reduced Weil form (constraint ↔ scaling by the idele class group; II₁,₁ ↔ the pole pair, signature (1,1); transverse V♮, positive definite ↔ H₊, missing; DDF operators and c = 26 ↔ no analogue yet). Middle: the four conditions on H₊. Bottom: how known candidates score against them; only the 𝔽_q model (primitive H² with the Hodge index theorem) passes all four.* [Cited + Analogy + Restatement; the four conditions are a Conjecture · 3.40 / 3.20 / 2.60 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![String side vs ζ side of the no-ghost theorem](figures/no_ghost/string_vs_zeta.png)', '', '*String side vs ζ side of the no-ghost theorem: Goddard–Thorn needs a Virasoro constraint, one Lorentzian II₁,₁ factor, a positive transverse factor, DDF operators and c = 26; for ζ the constraint (scaling) and the Lorentzian part (pole pair) exist, the transverse positivity is missing, and DDF and c = 26 have no analogue. Lower panel: ghost counts of the Weil form per window (Davenport–Heilbronn and the Beurling control acquire negative-norm states, two per off-line quadruple; ζ and 11a none in the tested range).* [Cited + Analogy + Numerical · 3.40 / 3.20 / 3.00]', '',
     '![Orbifolds and the mass shell](figures/no_ghost/orbifold_mass_shell.png)', '', '*Orbifolds and the mass shell: (1) V♮ as the FLM ℤ/2 orbifold of the Leech lattice VOA, 2²⁴ fixed points, 2¹² twisted ground states of weight 3/2, 98580 + 98304 = 196884; (2) the modular orbifold ℍ/SL₂(ℤ) with cone points i, ρ (CM discriminants −4, −3), the cusp cylinder, horocycles and the Frobenius (up) / Verschiebung (down) moves; (3) Jacobi constant, zero-velocity curves and Lagrange points, and the critical line with Davenport–Heilbronn’s off-line zeros as ghosts.* [Theorem + Cited + Analogy · 3.20 / 3.32 / 2.20]', '',
     '![Is F/V an archimedean/non-archimedean split? No](figures/no_ghost/newton_polygon_vs_infinity.png)', '', '*Is F/V an archimedean/non-archimedean split? No: both live at p. For Δ the Newton polygon of x² − τ(p)x + p¹¹ has slopes 0 and 11 at ordinary primes (all p < 60 except 2, 3, 5, 7), so the roots split by p-adic size; at ∞ the same pair has equal size |α| = |β| = p^{11/2} (Deligne) and β = ᾱ.* [Theorem · 3.00 / 3.32]', '',
     '![Animation](figures/no_ghost/z2_orbifold_fold.gif)', '', '*Animation: the ℤ/2 fold of the torus and the twisted sector appearing at the fixed points (schematic of the FLM construction of V♮).* [Cited, schematic]', '',
     '## The missing piece (refined in v1.11)',
     '',
     '> **The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.** This section states what is missing as precisely as the current evidence allows. Labels in brackets.',
     '',
     '**Statement [Open; any exact form is equivalent to RH, so it is stated as a construction requirement].** What is missing over ℤ is a *global, Hecke-equivariant, Krein-definite polarization* for ζ: a Rosati-type structure, built from the primes, the archimedean place and the pole together, that makes the scaling pencil D v̇ = Z_L v Krein-definite (the trivial grading Γ = 1, not merely some grading) at every support length L at once. By the Hamiltonian–Krein index n₋(Z_L) = N_real + 2N_quart + 2N_Krein− this is exactly Z_L ≥ 0, i.e. RH, so **the statement is circular as a criterion**; its non-circular content is the shape any construction must have [Theorem for the index identity; the requirements below are Conjecture]. (i) *Global:* ζ’s Euler factors carry no rotation (each sits on the parabolic wall, massless), the archimedean place is a one-sided cone, and every local term is indefinite (items 4, 11, 13). (ii) *Hecke-equivariant:* a single modular-invariant observable (the Epstein zeta of x² + 5y²) fails positivity at L* = 3.025 while the class-group-averaged L-functions do not, and log p enters only as the Hecke angle θ_p = t log p (item 15) [Numerical + Theorem]. (iii) *Coherent:* every local wall is already on the right side (Hasse, Ramanujan); what is unproved is that one spectral parameter γ makes the angles θ_p = γ log p coherent across all p with a definite archimedean sign. (iv) *Margin-free:* ζ is critical along L (Szegő), t (Rodgers–Tao) and the archimedean direction, so no uniform spectral gap exists (item 12). Over 𝔽_q all four are supplied by the polarization (Rosati, Castelnuovo–Severi, the Hodge index theorem), which makes every Frobenius mode Krein-definite and the Routh wall unreachable (Krein–Gelfand–Lidskii) [Theorem]. Over ℤ such a structure would presumably live on Deninger’s conjectured cohomology [Conjecture]. None of the analogies below constructs it.',
     '',
     '**What is now settled about it.**',
     '',
     '1. **Frame, not definiteness [Theorem].** The product formula (the pole plane) gives the Weil form Z = Pole + W_∞ − Σ_p W_p its Lorentzian (1,1) frame. On span{pole, g} positivity is the reverse Cauchy–Schwarz inequality of timelike vectors, the inequality behind the twin paradox. The frame does not make the form definite. The pole is the positive term at imaginary momentum r = ±i/2 that lifts the r ≈ 0 channel [Analogy].',
     '2. **Trichotomy and Weil radius [Restatement].** Along the support filtration each Levinson/Schur step has discriminant 1 − k²; over 𝔽_q exactly 1 − k₁² = (4q − a²)/4q, so elliptic / parabolic / hyperbolic ⇔ a² <, =, > 4q (a lattice Klein–Gordon mode with m² = 4 sin²(θ/2), item 13). The Weil radius L* is the first support length at which a direction turns negative, and **RH ⇔ L* = ∞ for ζ**. Under RH ζ is strictly elliptic at every finite L and meets the parabolic wall only as L → ∞; Davenport–Heilbronn crosses at L* ≈ 3.82–3.88 [Numerical].',
     '3. **Special-relativity dictionary [Theorem; circular as a criterion].** Each step is an SU(1,1) boost with velocity k: 1 − k² = 1/γ², the Lorentz factors multiply and the rapidities add. Positivity ⇔ every step is subluminal (Schur). ζ’s largest step up to L = 8 is |k| = 0.384; Davenport–Heilbronn reaches |k| ≥ 1 at L = 3.85, exactly at its wall (h = 0.025; ≈ 3.63 on fine bases, see Note (v1.14)) [Numerical]. Under RH the cumulative ratio Π(1 − k²) → 0 (Szegő): subluminal at every step, massless in the limit [Theorem, conditional on RH].',
     '4. **No local positivity, and no local rotation [Numerical + Theorem].** Every local term W_∞, W_p is indefinite, and partial products over places stay negative until every prime power ≤ e^L is present; the repair is collective. It is stronger than indefiniteness: ζ’s local factors carry no rotation at all (N = (F − V)/2 = 0, m² = 0 at every p), so ζ has no local carrier of the 𝔽_q mechanism.',
     '5. **Where the ghost lives [Numerical].** On Davenport–Heilbronn’s ghost 2-plane span{g, Dg} (taken at its wall) the archimedean part is positive definite. ζ’s prime terms keep that 2×2 positive at every partial sum, in either order and at both resolutions; Davenport–Heilbronn’s own terms break it only late, first after 73–100% of the terms. The ghost comes from the prime side.',
     '6. **One global polarization [Numerical; equivalent to n₋ = 0].** In the gyroscopic Weil pencil every ζ mode is imaginary with Krein sign +, and one fixed positive-frequency (Hardy) polarization, a Dirac sea, fills for ζ at every L ≤ 8; Davenport–Heilbronn leaves n₋/2 unfillable holes. In the language of item 9 the fill is Krein-definiteness, Γ = 1.',
     '7. **Shear, not curvature [Theorem + Numerical; equivalent to Laguerre].** The curvature of log|Ξ| is the Riesz measure 2πΣδ_ρ ≥ 0, the same for every function; the separating part is the trace-free shear. A pointwise Witten-type curvature condition fails, so positivity must be an operator inequality.',
     '8. **The sum of squares, explicitly, and where it breaks [Theorem + Numerical; circular as a criterion].** Toeplitz Cholesky is the Levinson recursion: Z(g) = Σ_k E_k |⟨a_k, g⟩|² with the Szegő predictor polynomials a_k. Positivity ⇔ every pivot E_k > 0 ⇔ (Schur–Cohn) no predictor root inside the unit disk. For ζ the roots lie on the circle at the zeta zeros; for Davenport–Heilbronn the root that enters the disk at the wall converges to the off-line zero itself. Π(1 − k²) is the transmitted fraction of a lossless layered line [Theorem].',
     '9. **Count versus stability, and the exact glue [Theorem + Numerical; circular as a criterion].** Morse counts are topologically forced (2 − 3 = −1 = χ for the co-rotating potential at every mass ratio; equal signed counts for ζ and Davenport–Heilbronn; χ(Tⁿ) = 0 on the prime torus), while stability is not. The two halves are joined exactly by the Hamiltonian–Krein index n₋(Z_L) = N_real + 2N_quart + 2N_Krein− and the Kelvin–Tait–Chetaev parity sign det(D⁻¹Z) = (−1)^{n₋}: an odd index forces a real pair and a gyroscopic twist rescues only even n₋ (212/212 pencils; odd n₋ ⇒ real pair 29/29). Hence positivity ⇔ stable **and** Krein-definite; a non-trivial grading exists for Davenport–Heilbronn on L ∈ [4.025, 4.625], so “some Γ” (v1.10) was too weak.',
     '10. **Two walls [Theorem + Numerical; the converse is open].** The *parabolic (signature) wall* is a one-mode collision of a conjugate pair on the real axis: a² = 4q over 𝔽_q, Kepler’s E = 0, a Levinson step with |k| = 1, a Hodge–Riemann signature flip; RH ⇔ ζ never reaches it [Restatement]. The *Routh (Krein-collision) wall* is a collision of two modes of opposite Krein sign off the real axis (Hamiltonian–Hopf; at L4, μ_R = 0.0385209). It needs a Krein-negative mode first, so RH ⇒ ζ never reaches any Routh wall, and under a Krein-definite (Rosati-type) polarization it is unreachable by Krein–Gelfand–Lidskii [Theorem]. Davenport–Heilbronn crosses the parabolic wall at L* = 3.875 (h = 0.025; ≈ 3.63/3.65 on fine bases) (the birth of a cap of the forbidden set), opens a Krein-negative neck at 4.025, and shows Hamiltonian–Hopf quartets from L_H ≈ 4.26–4.78 (by resolution); ζ shows none of this for L ≤ 8 [Numerical]. Whether every Krein-negative mode must reach a Routh wall as L → ∞ is open [Conjecture]. In the de Bruijn–Newman direction ζ sits exactly on its wall: RH ⇔ Λ = 0 given Λ ≥ 0 (Rodgers–Tao) [Theorem, cited]; the reading “forward heat flow plays the role of the Coriolis stiffness” is demoted in v1.11 (Analogy 2.4 → 2.2), since that flow is a gradient flow with a Calogero–Moser lift, not gyroscopic.',
     '11. **One-sided archimedean cone, two-sided prime rigidity [Theorem + Numerical; Conjecture].** π sech(πr) = Re ψ(3/4 + ir/2) − Re ψ(1/4 + ir/2) > 0, so raising the archimedean multiplier never breaks positivity, while every finite prime-side change has a mean-zero symbol and breaks it at a finite cutoff: ζ sits on the boundary of the Weil cone in every prime direction. Conjecture: the grading is the supporting functional of the positive cone at the prime–archimedean pairing.',
     '12. **Criticality: no margin [Theorem (conditional) + Cited + Heuristic].** Genuine L-functions are gapless in three directions: Szegő’s condition fails along L under RH, Λ ≥ 0 along the heat flow (Rodgers–Tao), and the archimedean factor is (conjecturally) minimal admissible. Arguments of the form “Z_L ≥ δ > 0 uniformly” cannot work; a proof must be identity-type (a sum of squares or a polarization), as Hasse is over 𝔽_q. Criticality separates genuine L-functions from Davenport–Heilbronn, not ζ from L(11a).',
     '13. **Hecke recursion = lattice Klein–Gordon [Theorem].** The local transfer matrix [[a/√q, −1], [1, 0]] is a lossless LC-ladder cell with m² = 2 − a/√q: Hasse = passband (543/543 pairs; L(11a) 429/429 in band, mean m² = 1.9997), the wall = band edge, a ghost = stopband. ζ sits at m² = 0 at every prime, the massless band edge, so ζ’s positivity cannot be a cascade of local passbands.',
     '14. **Real-field thermodynamics is RH-blind [Theorem + Restatement].** For any positive kernel Φ, log ∫Φ e^{xu} is convex in real x (Hölder), so entropy, heat capacity and the TS ↔ PV correspondence hold for Davenport–Heilbronn too (its kernel is positive and log-concave). The RH-sensitive quantity is the imaginary-field (rotation) one: −∂² log H_t is Laguerre’s expression, ≥ 0 exactly when the zeros are real (circular as a test).',
     '15. **The elliptic-curve analogue on the discriminant space [Theorem + Numerical; Conjecture].** On binary quadratic forms, definite forms are CM points (elliptic), D = 0 is the cusp (parabolic) and indefinite forms are closed geodesics (hyperbolic), with |tr| <, =, > 2 the same trichotomy as a² vs 4q. Modular invariance alone is not enough: the single-form Epstein zeta is modular and fails, the Hecke-equivariant (class-group-averaged) objects hold. log p enters the modular surface only as the Hecke angle θ_p = t log p (Eisenstein eigenvalue 2cos(t log p)); no time change maps geodesic lengths 2 log ε_D to k log p (Livšic), and the Hecke-tree band edge |λ| = 2√p is the parabolic wall again. RH ⇔ the scattering resonances ρ/2 all sit at the same depth ¼ from the unitary line [Restatement].',
     '',
     '**Closest literal version, and the gap.** Over 𝔽_q every item above is literal: Rosati positivity = Hasse = a definite Frobenius form = a passband Klein–Gordon mode = a subluminal first step = a Krein-definite rotation that never meets a Routh wall. Over ℤ each item is either a restatement of RH or a finite-cutoff observation. The gap is exactly the global polarization: a non-circular construction, from the primes, ∞ and the pole together, of a Hecke-equivariant, Krein-definite (Γ = 1) structure whose angles θ_p are coherent across all p with a definite archimedean sign — the structure whose presence the numerics show for ζ and whose absence they show for Davenport–Heilbronn and for single-form Epstein zetas. Since v1.16 rank 1 is the polarization entry, the computed-priority statement of this gap; the earlier pinned Φ entry is kept below at its computed priority.',
     '',
     '**Note (v1.12).** The product formula fixes the arithmetic prime coupling at κ = 1, but positivity is a separate input: ζ stays inside an exponentially narrowing window of admissible couplings around κ = 1 while Davenport–Heilbronn leaves it, and this separation is Weil positivity restated, so it is circular as a criterion [Numerical]. The statement above is unchanged from v1.11.',
     '',
     '**Note (v1.13).** The same one-negative frame appears on the Selberg side: for PSL₂(ℤ) the trace-formula form with the continuous spectrum removed has exactly one negative direction, in the pole plane span{e^{±x/2}}, as ζ’s Weil form does with the pole removed. There the complement is positive because the Laplacian is self-adjoint (Selberg); for ζ that complement positivity is RH [Numerical + Restatement]. What transfers through the explicit formula is the frame (the pole plane, its flip and the s ↔ 1 − s boost), not the positivity. The statement above is unchanged.',
     '',
     '**Note (v1.14).** Sharpened: positivity of Maass–Selberg type lives only in the doubled variable (Re 2s = 1, φ = ξ(2s−1)/ξ(2s)). On the weight-½ unitary axis (S = s + k/4) ζ’s own line appears only as zeros of a Fourier coefficient, c₁ ∝ ζ(½+2it)/ζ(1+4it), which the norm sees squared [Theorem]; every theta-lift rung tested keeps one doubling [Theorem for the rungs tested; Conjecture for all lifts]. What is missing is therefore a positivity that is linear in the coefficients c_m(S) off the unitary axis, as Weil’s criterion is a family of linear conditions, one per test function [Open]. Davenport–Heilbronn has no theta realization, so no such norm can be formed for it; only the Weil form sees it. **Correction:** Davenport–Heilbronn’s first Weil-window crossings are L ≈ 3.63/3.65 on fine bases (second pair ≈ 4.19/4.21); the values 3.82–3.90 (and 6.3–7.4) quoted in this section and in earlier galleries are hat-width (h = 0.025) basis values. The statement above is unchanged.',
     '',
     '## Previous results (v1.14)', '', 'The headline figure of v1.14 and the figures from the research notes folded into v1.14 (see README.md for the gallery and CHANGELOG.md for details). The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.', '',
     '![The sharpened missing piece: one doubling per rung](figures/hero_v114/missing_piece_one_doubling.png)', '', '*The sharpened missing piece (research notes of 2–3 Nov 2026). (A) Maass–Selberg positivity lives in the doubled variable: the truncated Eisenstein norm reads ζ through φ = ξ(2s−1)/ξ(2s), at Re 2s = 1. (B) On the weight-½ unitary axis S = s + k/4 = ½ + it, ζ’s own line appears only as zeros of a Fourier coefficient, c₁ ∝ ζ(½+2it)/ζ(1+4it), which the norm contains squared, so it is blind to where the zeros are. (C) Every theta-lift rung tested keeps one doubling. (D) The missing piece is a positivity linear in the coefficients c_m(S) off the unitary axis, as Weil’s criterion is a family of linear conditions, one per test function (schematic) [Open]. (E) Davenport–Heilbronn has no theta realization; only the Weil form sees it, with first crossings at L ≈ 3.63/3.65 on fine bases (3.85–3.90 at h = 0.025 was a hat-width artefact). (F) Index-1 Jacobi forms move the square r² into the index D = 4n − r²; at D = 0, H(0) = ζ(−1) = −1/12 = χ(SL₂(ℤ)), boundary data only.* [Theorem + Numerical; one doubling per rung is a Conjecture for all lifts; the missing piece is Open · 3.54 / 3.36 / 2.52 (scores 0–4)] Animation: figures/hero_v114/missing_piece_one_doubling.mp4 (MP4), figures/hero_v114/missing_piece_one_doubling.gif (GIF).', '',
     '![Weight ½ on Γ₀(4)](figures/weight_half/plus_space_maass_selberg.png)', '', '*Weight ½ on Γ₀(4): Eisenstein coefficients from Kloosterman–Gauss sums agree with Goldfeld–Hoffstein Cor. 1.3 (left); on the unitary axis S = ½ + it the plus-space Maass–Selberg diagonal M⁺_T(t) = 2·M^{SL₂}_{2√T}(2t) stays positive while the m = 1 coefficient |ζ(½+2it)/ζ(1+4it)| vanishes at t = γ/2 (right).* [Numerical + Theorem · 3.00 / 3.54 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![One doubling per rung](figures/weight_half/one_doubling_per_rung.png)', '', '*One doubling per rung: on each object’s unitary axis the norm or inner product evaluates ζ or L at the edge Re 1; ζ’s line appears only linearly in a Fourier coefficient (weight ½) or as a square |L(½+it)|² with no sign information (Rallis boundary case).* [Theorem for the rungs tested, Conjecture for all lifts · 3.36 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Maass–Selberg truncated norm for SL₂(ℤ)](figures/maass_selberg/truncated_norm.png)', '', '*Maass–Selberg truncated norm for SL₂(ℤ): positive for every T ≥ 1, verified by direct integration over the truncated fundamental domain; it reads ζ only at the doubled variable 2s.* [Numerical · 2.56 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Spectral flow of the nested window family (M = 320)](figures/window_flow/window_eigenflow.png)', '', '*Spectral flow of the nested window family (M = 320): eigenvalues are non-increasing in L, every system sinks into a near-null floor, ζ stays non-negative, ζ with the pole removed has its single negative from L ≈ 0.22, and Davenport–Heilbronn’s first pair leaves the floor at L ≈ 3.63/3.65 (h = 0.025 gave 3.85–3.90, a hat-width artefact).* [Theorem + Numerical · 2.80 / 3.20 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '## Previous results (v1.13)', '', 'The v1.13 headline figure and the figures from the research notes folded into v1.13. The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.', '',
     '![The missing piece: one timelike direction](figures/hero_v113/missing_piece_one_timelike_direction.png)', '', '*The missing piece in the analogies of 1 Nov 2026: one timelike direction is understood, positivity of everything else is not. Independent Euler factors (k-fold orbits of length k log p, no p–q coupling) feed the explicit-formula Weil form; with the pole removed it has exactly one negative direction, the pole/cusp plane span{e^{±x/2}}, read three ways (the symmetrised product of a forward and a backward shift vacancy at rate ½, the ± Krein collision into a Jordan block at the parabolic edge |t| = 2, the L1 saddle whose fundamental symmetry is time reversal). On the Selberg side (PSL₂(ℤ)) the same frame holds and the complement is positive because the Laplacian is self-adjoint; for ζ that complement positivity is RH. What transfers through the explicit formula is the frame, not the positivity. The missing piece is a canonical, flow-invariant J, equivalently an independently positive Gram realization Z_L = G_L*G_L built without the zeros; over 𝔽_q it exists (Rosati / Hodge index), for ζ it would presumably live on Deninger’s conjectured cohomology [Open].* [Theorem + Numerical; the missing piece is Open · 3.00 / 2.20 (scores 0–4)] Animation: figures/hero_v113/missing_piece_one_timelike_direction.mp4 (MP4), figures/hero_v113/missing_piece_one_timelike_direction.gif (GIF).', '',
     '![ζ’s Weil form with and without the pole](figures/lorentzian_frame/pole_removed_signatures.png)', '', '*ζ’s Weil form with and without the pole: the full form is positive at every tested window L ≤ 8, and with the pole removed it has exactly one negative direction (n−1, 1), 99.9% in the pole plane span{e^{±x/2}}; Davenport–Heilbronn and a Beurling control gain negative directions near their off-line points, one per real off-line point and two per complex quadruple.* [Numerical + Theorem · 3.40 / 3.32 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![PSL₂(ℤ), Selberg side](figures/lorentzian_frame/selberg_lorentzian.png)', '', '*PSL₂(ℤ), Selberg side: the cusp-form part of the trace-formula form is positive (Selberg’s theorem, checked), geometric minus continuous has exactly one negative direction, converging to the pole plane, and dropping the continuous (cusp) term breaks the frame; the frame transfers to ζ through the explicit formula, the positivity does not.* [Numerical; Restatement · 3.00 / 2.20 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Over 𝔽_q the Weil form is the Gram matrix of the Frobenius orbit in H¹ for the Rosati / Hodge-index form](figures/function_field_gram/ff_weil_gram.png)', '', '*Over 𝔽_q the Weil form is the Gram matrix of the Frobenius orbit in H¹ for the Rosati / Hodge-index form: rank 2g and signature (1, ρ−1) on all 25 tested curves, while ghost (non-Weil) data break the Hodge index; no place-by-place positive star exists for ζ.* [Theorem · 3.70 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![LRL vector against the Jacobi constant](figures/lrl_jacobi/lrl_C_frame_tidal.png)', '', '*LRL vector against the Jacobi constant: {A, C} splits exactly into a frame (Coriolis) part, which survives μ → 0, and a tidal part ∝ μ²; near L4 the Routh instability shows only in the trace, since a traceless 2×2 form is always indefinite.* [Theorem + Numerical; as an analogue of trace-free positivity, Analogy · 2.60 / 1.80 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '## Previous results (v1.12)', '', 'The v1.11 headline figure and the figures from the research notes folded into v1.12. The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.', '',
     '![One integrable torus, one quadratic form, two walls](figures/hero_v111/prime_torus_two_walls.png)', '', '*One integrable torus, one quadratic form, two walls: (a) the Euler product as commuting rotations on the prime torus (θ_p = t log p, a Kronecker line, non-resonant by unique factorization); (b) local factors as quadratic forms x² + a xy + q y² in the discriminant plane: the curve 11a strictly inside at all 429 primes, ζ’s Euler factors on the parabolic wall a² = 4q, a ghost (q = 7, a = 6) outside; (c) the global Weil form along the support length: ζ’s smallest eigenvalue stays positive with no margin (3·10⁻¹⁰ → 10⁻¹²), while Davenport–Heilbronn crosses the parabolic wall at L* = 3.875, opens a Krein-negative neck at 4.025 and reaches the Routh wall (Hamiltonian–Hopf quartets) from 4.263–4.775. Animation: figures/hero_v111/prime_torus_two_walls.mp4 (18 s) and .gif.* [Theorem + Numerical · data from the research notes]', '',
     '![Three normalizations of the Euler data as lattice axes](figures/normalizations/three_normalizations.png)', '', '*Three normalizations of the Euler data as lattice axes. |a| (the infinite-place norm of the trace, untied from p by the product formula) forces the trace to ±1, keeps only the sign of a² − 4q, breaks at supersingular primes and gives a Gram form that is indefinite from the first window; √q gives the local Hasse lattice, which is positive for L(11a), degenerate for ζ·ζ and blind to Davenport–Heilbronn, and without the archimedean factor every window is indefinite; √q together with W∞ is the Weil form, where ζ stays inside an exponentially pinching prime-coupling window around κ = 1 while Davenport–Heilbronn and a Beurling control leave it. The last separation is Weil positivity restated (circular).* [Theorem + Numerical; circular · 3.16 / 3.28 / 3.48 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Drag at L4 and L5](figures/trojans_drag/drag_growth.png)', '', '*Drag at L4 and L5: any velocity damping destabilises the Krein-negative libration for every μ below the Routh value (Kelvin–Tait–Chetaev), with L4 and L5 exactly isospectral under symmetric damping; drag relative to a sub-Keplerian gas flow breaks the symmetry and makes L4 less unstable than L5.* [Theorem + Numerical · 3.40 / 2.84 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Massive Trojans and the Routh criterion](figures/trojans_drag/massive_trojans.png)', '', '*Massive Trojans and the Routh criterion: the generalized criterion 27(m₁m₂ + m₂m₃ + m₃m₁) < M² holds on every tested triple; Sun–Jupiter would need about 9–41 Jupiter masses of Trojans to lose stability (the actual Trojan mass is about 3·10⁻¹¹ solar masses); a uniform positive background density raises the Routh limit to 27μ(1 − μ) < (1 + 4κ)².* [Numerical + Theorem · 3.04 / 3.08 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '## Previous results (v1.11)', '', 'Figures from the research notes folded into v1.11. The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.', '',
     '![Hamiltonian–Krein index for the Weil pencil](figures/krein_index/hamiltonian_krein_index_dh.png)', '', '*Hamiltonian–Krein index for the Weil pencil: Davenport–Heilbronn’s n₋(Z_L) splits as N_real + 2N_quart + 2N_Krein− at every support length (a real pair at the parabolic wall, a rescued Krein-negative window, then Routh quartets); the identity and the Kelvin–Tait–Chetaev parity hold at 212/212 pencils for ζ, Davenport–Heilbronn, L(11a) and a hybrid. Positivity ⇔ stable and Krein-definite (Γ = 1).* [Theorem + Numerical · 4.00 (scores 0–4; circular as a criterion)]', '',
     '![One observer versus all observers at discriminant −20](figures/discriminant_space/observers_deuring.png)', '', '*One observer versus all observers at discriminant −20: the Epstein zeta of the single form x² + 5y² (off-line zero 0.93297 + 15.66825i) fails positivity at L* = 3.025, while the class-group-averaged ζ_K and the genus L(χ₋₄)L(χ₅) stay positive to L = 8; Deuring’s count of curves over 𝔽_p by trace is exact.* [Numerical + Theorem · 2.8 / 3.1 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![The space of binary quadratic forms](figures/discriminant_space/discriminant_space.png)', '', '*The space of binary quadratic forms: reduced definite forms are CM points (elliptic), D = 0 is the cusp (parabolic), Zagier-reduced indefinite forms are closed geodesics of length 2 log ε_D (hyperbolic).* [Theorem · 3.1 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Prime geodesic theorem for SL₂(ℤ) (π_Γ/Li = 0.9976 at 10⁶), the periodogram peaking at the Maass eigenvalues, and the Sundman test](figures/discriminant_space/prime_geodesics_sundman.png)', '', '*Prime geodesic theorem for SL₂(ℤ) (π_Γ/Li = 0.9976 at 10⁶), the periodogram peaking at the Maass eigenvalues, and the Sundman test: the counts match the primes but the lengths come in blocks (998 distinct lengths), so no time change maps them to log p.* [Numerical + Theorem · 2.9 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Hecke trees](figures/discriminant_space/tree_kesten_mckay.png)', '', '*Hecke trees: Kesten–McKay densities with the band edge |λ| = 2√p (zero group velocity, the parabolic wall again); 70 supersingular 2-isogeny graphs, all Ramanujan; discrete Morse counts grow like n/2 (no structural five).* [Theorem + Analogy · 2.4 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Jacobi (potential) half, Legendre (symplectic) half and the exact index theorem that joins them](figures/krein_index/legendre_jacobi_twist.png)', '', '*Jacobi (potential) half, Legendre (symplectic) half and the exact index theorem that joins them.* [Theorem · 4.00 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Mass and the walls](figures/krein_index/higgs_trichotomy_walls.png)', '', '*Mass and the walls: Landau bowl / critical / hilltop; the Hecke recursion as a lattice Klein–Gordon ladder (L(11a) masses inside the band, ζ at m² = 0); the archimedean symbol Ω(r) negative below r₀ = 6.2898; Szegő innovations; Penning-trap Krein signs.* [Theorem + Analogy · 3.76 / 3.64 / 2.88 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Routh versus Hasse](figures/legendre_jacobi/routh_vs_hasse.png)', '', '*Routh versus Hasse: in the rotating frame the L4 Hessian is negative definite and stability comes from the Coriolis twist up to μ_R = 0.0385209; over 𝔽_q the Frobenius rotation N = (F − V)/2 with N² = (a² − 4q)/4 is kept off the wall by Rosati, so a Routh wall cannot occur.* [Theorem + Numerical · 4.2 (0.6·sig + 0.4·prob, each on 0–5)]', '',
     '![Frobenius and Verschiebung as rotation plus scalar](figures/legendre_jacobi/fv_decomposition.png)', '', '*Frobenius and Verschiebung as rotation plus scalar: (F + V)/2 = (a/2)·I and N = (F − V)/2; ζ’s local factors carry no rotation.* [Theorem · 4.1 (each on 0–5)]', '',
     '![Hill regions of the Weil form](figures/legendre_jacobi/weil_hill_regions.png)', '', '*Hill regions of the Weil form: the conserved energy vᵀZv sees only signature events (the forbidden set and its Euler characteristic), not the Krein data.* [Theorem + Numerical · 3.7 (each on 0–5)]', '',
     '![The two arms](figures/legendre_jacobi/two_arms.png)', '', '*The two arms: Legendre (symplectic, Krein signs, Routh wall) versus Jacobi (potential, Hill regions, signature events); the gap over ℚ is that the archimedean side is a one-sided cone, not a rotation.* [Theorem · 3.8 (each on 0–5)]', '',
     '![Why the archimedean side has a one-sided cone and the prime side has none](figures/archimedean_cone/symbols_one_sided_cone.png)', '', '*Why the archimedean side has a one-sided cone and the prime side has none: Re ψ(3/4 + ir/2) − Re ψ(1/4 + ir/2) = π sech(πr) > 0, while every finite prime-side symbol is a mean-zero trigonometric polynomial.* [Theorem · 3.4 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![ζ sits on the boundary of the Weil cone in every non-positive direction](figures/archimedean_cone/radius_all_directions.png)', '', '*ζ sits on the boundary of the Weil cone in every non-positive direction: the radius of positivity decays like λ_min(Z_L).* [Numerical · 3.28 (scores 0–4; circular core)]', '',
     '![Two failure channels](figures/archimedean_cone/failure_channels.png)', '', '*Two failure channels: the first negative direction sits at spectral frequency 0 (archimedean factor against the pole) or at r ≈ 5.5–8.4 (archimedean factor against the primes).* [Numerical · 2.96 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![The Euler product is not sufficient](figures/euler_routh/beurling_control.png)', '', '*The Euler product is not sufficient: Beurling systems with an Euler product, non-negative prime measure and unique factorization cross both walls.* [Numerical · 3.68 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![The natural home of unique factorization](figures/euler_routh/fock_torus.png)', '', '*The natural home of unique factorization: a non-resonant tensor product of oscillators (H = Σ log p · N_p, Tr e^{−sH} = ζ), against the Hilbert monoid (441 = 9·49 = 21²) and the maximally resonant Kepler problem.* [Theorem · 3.4 / 3.44 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![ζ is on the boundary of the Weil cone in every prime direction](figures/euler_routh/prime_rigidity.png)', '', '*ζ is on the boundary of the Weil cone in every prime direction: a missing prime or a 0.3% change of one weight is detected as soon as it enters the window.* [Numerical · 3.16 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![The KS lift of both walls](figures/euler_routh/ks_two_walls.png)', '', '*The KS lift of both walls: Kepler becomes a definite oscillator (only the parabolic wall), the lifted L4 keeps its Routh collision at μ_R.* [Theorem · 3.44 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Saddle-chain Melnikov pairing](figures/discriminant_space/melnikov_pairing.png)', '', '*Saddle-chain Melnikov pairing: the Weil form on modulated bumps stays positive for ζ and turns negative near the off-line zeros of Davenport–Heilbronn and of an Epstein zeta (a zero detector, circular).* [Restatement + Numerical · 2.6 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![The prime torus](figures/discriminant_space/torus_small_divisors_morse.png)', '', '*The prime torus: small divisors |Σ k_p log p| behave like random frequencies (Diophantine, RH-blind), and critical-point counts have alternating sum χ(Tⁿ) = 0 in every case.* [Numerical + Theorem · 2.3 / 2.5 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Real-field thermodynamics is convex for any positive kernel](figures/thermo_dbn/kernel_entropies.png)', '', '*Real-field thermodynamics is convex for any positive kernel: Davenport–Heilbronn’s kernel Φ is positive and log-concave too, so entropy and heat capacity cannot separate ζ from a counterexample.* [Theorem · 3.4 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![The de Bruijn–Newman zero flow as a log-gas with sum-of-squares entropy production while the zeros are real](figures/thermo_dbn/zero_flow.png)', '', '*The de Bruijn–Newman zero flow as a log-gas with sum-of-squares entropy production while the zeros are real; its second-order lift is Calogero–Moser (Hamiltonian), so the flow is not conformally symplectic.* [Theorem · 3.5 / 3.2 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '## Previous results (v1.10)', '', 'Figures from the research notes folded into v1.10. The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.', '',
     '![Two walls, not one](figures/two_walls/two_walls_dictionary.png)', '', '*Two walls, not one: the parabolic (signature) wall, where a conjugate pair collides on the real axis (Hasse a² = 4q, Kepler E = 0, Levinson |k| = 1, Hodge index change; RH ⇔ ζ never reaches it), and the downstream Routh (Krein-collision) wall at μ_R = 0.0385209; Davenport–Heilbronn passes the first at L*, sits in an indefinite-but-elliptic window, then shows Hamiltonian–Hopf quartets; in the de Bruijn–Newman direction ζ sits exactly on its wall.* [Theorem + Numerical · 3.52 (0.6·sig + 0.4·prob, 0–4)]', '',
     '![Positivity concepts (Weil, Rosati, Laguerre–Pólya, Hodge–Riemann, product formula, ferromagnetic Φ, Routh wall) against the mechanics pictures (Kepler/KS, shear/Raychaudhuri, Lagrange/Krein, special relativity, Routh wall)](figures/two_walls/positivity_crosstable.png)', '', '*Positivity concepts (Weil, Rosati, Laguerre–Pólya, Hodge–Riemann, product formula, ferromagnetic Φ, Routh wall) against the mechanics pictures (Kepler/KS, shear/Raychaudhuri, Lagrange/Krein, special relativity, Routh wall): the most exact correspondence in each cell, with its label and score; every cell at 3.4 or above is a finite-dimensional theorem or an RH-equivalent restatement.* [Theorem / Restatement / Analogy · cells 2.0–3.6 (0.6·sig + 0.4·prob, 0–4)]', '',
     '![Davenport–Heilbronn’s ghost 2-plane](figures/relativity_shear/dh_ghost_plane_2x2.png)', '', '*Davenport–Heilbronn’s ghost 2-plane: the archimedean part is positive there; ζ’s prime terms never break the 2×2 (100% of partial sums, in either order), while Davenport–Heilbronn’s own terms break it late. The ghost comes from the prime side.* [Numerical · 3.2 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Levinson/Schur steps are SU(1,1) boosts](figures/relativity_shear/levinson_boosts_rapidity.png)', '', '*Levinson/Schur steps are SU(1,1) boosts: ζ stays subluminal (max |k| = 0.384 up to L = 8), Davenport–Heilbronn’s first |k| ≥ 1 is its light-cone crossing at L = 3.85, Lorentz factors multiply, and over 𝔽_q k₁ = −a/(2√q).* [Theorem + Numerical · 3.5, circular as a criterion (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![The positivity cup at E = 0](figures/positivity_cup/positivity_bowl_flat_peak.png)', '', '*The positivity cup at E = 0: bowl, flat and peak are definite, semidefinite and indefinite (null set a point, a line, an X); ζ’s smallest Weil eigenvalue flattens but stays positive, while Davenport–Heilbronn flips to a peak at L* ≈ 3.82.* [Theorem + Numerical · 3.0 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![KS trichotomy](figures/positivity_cup/ks_trichotomy_sundman.png)', '', '*KS trichotomy: oscillator (E < 0), free particle (E = 0, the null line is the wall) and inverted oscillator = xp (E > 0, the null X); Sundman time makes Kepler’s radial motion linear; the e^{t/2} normalization turns the decaying Mellin spiral into the unit circle.* [Theorem · 3.4; normalization panel 2.8 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Shear, not curvature](figures/relativity_shear/raychaudhuri_shear.png)', '', '*Shear, not curvature: σ² − ω² = E/2 for the KS oscillator; Routh stability at L4 in tidal and rotation variables; the trace-free Hessian of log|Ξ| keeps its sign on the line for ζ and flips at Davenport–Heilbronn’s ghost heights, while the curvature is blind off the zeros.* [Theorem + Numerical · 3.5, equivalent to Laguerre (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Dirac sea and curvature density](figures/relativity_shear/dirac_sea_curvature_density.png)', '', '*Dirac sea and curvature density: one global positive-frequency sea fills for ζ, Davenport–Heilbronn leaves n₋/2 holes; curvature per unit kinetic energy sits on the edge −1 for ζ and below it for Davenport–Heilbronn; the Riesz curvature density is ≥ 0 for both.* [Numerical + Theorem · 3.3 / 3.4, circular as a criterion (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Twin paradox at the Lagrange points](figures/relativity_shear/lagrange_point_clocks.png)', '', '*Twin paradox at the Lagrange points: the co-rotating clock rate is the Jacobi constant, so the Lagrange points are its critical points and L4/L5 clocks run fastest; L4 and L5 split only when time reversal is broken.* [Theorem + Numerical · 3.6 / 3.2 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Single orbit vs ensemble](figures/positivity_cup/single_orbit_vs_ensemble.png)', '', '*Single orbit vs ensemble: over 𝔽_q the ensemble inertia is the per-pair Hasse data aggregated (122/122); in the restricted three-body problem a planet clears a chaotic band but the co-orbital Trojan and horseshoe ring survives completely.* [Theorem + Numerical · 3.4 / 3.2 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Frame dependence](figures/positivity_cup/frame_dependence_krein.png)', '', '*Frame dependence: rotating and inertial frames give the same Floquet multipliers and Krein signs (to 5e−12); only the picture changes. For ζ the Weil pencil is Hamiltonian only in the e^{t/2}-normalized frame (100% against 6.7% imaginary).* [Theorem + Numerical · 3.4 / 3.0 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Librations and polarization](figures/librations_krein/polarization_coherency_local_global.png)', '', '*Librations and polarization: the genus-1 Hasse bound is a positive coherency (Stokes) matrix (1800/1800 curves); Levinson steps as 2×2 coherencies; L4 modes as polarization ellipses whose Krein collision is a polarization collision; place-by-place and pole-plane filtrations (the edge-prime reading is refuted in v1.10).* [Theorem + Numerical · 3.4 / 3.3; pole plane 1.6 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![L4/L5 and the Weil form](figures/librations_krein/l4_routh_krein_rosati_ks.png)', '', '*L4/L5 and the Weil form: Krein signs and the Routh wall at μ_R = 0.0385; a Rosati-like involution is a Krein-signed complex structure; the gyroscopic Weil pencil is all-imaginary with Krein + for ζ and L4-like for Davenport–Heilbronn; the KS lift reads the Kepler trichotomy as the sign of b² − ac, with xp on the hyperbolic side.* [Theorem + Numerical · 3.4 / 3.4 / 3.3 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Hill regions and the prime-threshold filtration](figures/librations_krein/hill_regions_thresholds_krein.png)', '', '*Hill regions and the prime-threshold filtration: every ζ threshold L = log n is regular; Krein spectroscopy; the equator crossing at a wall; the Bernoulli numbers fix the archimedean sign change r* = 2π + 0.0067; the Möbius light cone.* [Numerical + Theorem · 3.3 / 3.2 / 2.9; Hill-region dictionary 1.5 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Apsides, escape directions, hodograph and the Levi-Civita map](figures/librations_krein/apsides_escape_directions.png)', '', '*Apsides, escape directions, hodograph and the Levi-Civita map: the escape directions are a conjugate pair that collides on the apsidal line at e = 1 and splits into two real null asymptotes for e > 1; the Frobenius quadratic is the focal quadratic of the conic.* [Theorem · 3.0 (scores 0–4)]', '',
     '![Place-by-place and support filtrations of the Weil form](figures/weil_trichotomy/support_filtration_trichotomy.png)', '', '*Place-by-place and support filtrations of the Weil form: partial products over places stay negative until every prime power ≤ e^L is present; Levinson steps; the literal light cone; Laguerre log-concavity on the line; the elliptic / parabolic / hyperbolic trichotomy with Weil radius L* ≈ 3.82 for Davenport–Heilbronn; over 𝔽_q 4q − a² = q·det T₂.* [Restatement + Numerical · trichotomy 3.92; local definiteness falsified 3.60 (0.6·sig (0–10) + 0.4·prob (0–1))]', '',
     '![The missing piece, refined](figures/sos_morse/missing_piece.png)', '', '*The missing piece, refined: the Levinson/Szegő sum-of-squares factor of the Weil form. ζ: roots on the circle at the zeros. Davenport–Heilbronn: the root that enters the disk is the off-line zero.* [Numerical · 3.6]', '',
     '![Witten-type best constant c(L) for several cushions](figures/sos_morse/sos_constants.png)', '', '*Witten-type best constant c(L) for several cushions: same sign in every variant (circular), ζ at 1 − O(1e-10).* [Restatement · 3.0]', '',
     '![The negative direction of the Davenport–Heilbronn Weil form lives at the frequency of its off-line zero](figures/sos_morse/soft_profiles.png)', '', '*The negative direction of the Davenport–Heilbronn Weil form lives at the frequency of its off-line zero.* [Numerical · 3.2]', '',
     '![Morse count of the co-rotating potential](figures/sos_morse/cr3bp_morse.png)', '', '*Morse count of the co-rotating potential: 2 − 3 = −1 = χ for every mass ratio.* [Theorem · 3.8]', '',
     '![Morse counting of log|Ξ|](figures/sos_morse/xi_morse.png)', '', '*Morse counting of log|Ξ|: off-line zeros force two extra saddles each on the critical line.* [Theorem · 3.4]', '',
     '![Spectral flow](figures/sos_morse/spectral_flow.png)', '', '*Spectral flow: the Morse index of the window Weil form along L.* [Numerical · 3.3]', '',
     '![Count versus stability](figures/sos_morse/count_vs_stability.png)', '', '*Count versus stability: topology fixes the count, the gyroscopic term (positivity) decides stability.* [Theorem · 3.5]', '',
     '## Previous results (v1.9)', '', 'Figures from the research notes folded into v1.9. The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.', '',
     '![Two-degree-of-freedom chaotic regularized xp](figures/regularized_xp/chaotic_two_dof_xp_orbit_lengths.png)', '', '*Two-degree-of-freedom chaotic regularized xp: periodic-orbit periods drift with energy and do not lock onto k·log p; modular-surface lengths count like the primes but are logs of quadratic units; the explicit-formula sign is cohomological, not a Maslov phase; ζ’s orbit sum improves with the prime cutoff while Davenport–Heilbronn’s degrades.* [Numerical + Theorem + Cited · 3.9 / 4.4 / 4.4 / 3.9 (as a route to RH 2.9; adelic orbit space 3.4)]', '',
     '![Regularized xp](figures/regularized_xp/regularized_xp_legendre_liouville_counting.png)', '', '*Regularized xp: a Poincaré time change with ∂f/∂p ≠ 0 makes xp Legendre-regular; the regularized xp is the hyperbolic Sundman clock of the F/V pair; in a Deninger-type toy the Liouville law gives only ρ ↔ 1 − ρ and a positive metric gives the line; counting matches only the two leading Riemann–von Mangoldt terms.* [Theorem + Numerical · 4.1 / 4.4 / 4.7 / 3.8 (as a route to RH 3.1)]', '',
     '## Previous results (v1.8)', '', 'Figures from the research notes folded into v1.8. The Riemann Hypothesis is not proved.', '',
     '![Central-charge positivity is the Weil form](figures/nc_torus_fv/central_charge_positivity_zeta_vs_dh.png)', '', '*Central-charge positivity is the Weil form: from the prime side ζ stays ≥ 0 up to L = 8, while Davenport–Heilbronn turns negative near L = 4, with no zeros used.* [Restatement + Numerical · route score 4.7 (as a route to RH 2.8)]', '',
     '![Zero-free signed energy of the heat-flowed Ξ](figures/heat_flow_energy/zero_free_signed_energy_trace.png)', '', '*Zero-free signed energy of the heat-flowed Ξ: every ghost goes negative before it lands, ζ windows stay positive, and the Hermite form counts off-line pairs exactly.* [Theorem + Numerical · 4.4 (as a route to RH 3.3)]', '',
     '![Hofstadter gap widths are log-linear in |n| and close like (distance to the wall)^½ on the lobe side](figures/nc_torus_fv/gap_widths_and_nonnormal_residue.png)', '', '*Hofstadter gap widths are log-linear in |n| and close like (distance to the wall)^½ on the lobe side; ‖P − P&#42;‖ is a gauge quantity that spikes at every collision, not a ghost test.* [Numerical + Theorem · 3.6 / 3.4 / 4.4 (as a ghost detector 1.6)]', '',
     '![F/V on the noncommutative torus](figures/nc_torus_fv/nc_torus_gap_labels_ghost_blind.png)', '', '*F/V on the noncommutative torus: Manin’s lobe / wall / wedge, and gap labels that hold for every zero set, so the K-theory order is ghost-blind.* [Theorem + Numerical · 4.7 (as a route to ζ positivity 2.8)]', '',
     '![The Rodgers–Tao energy is an exact Sundman clock with speed limit 4, but monotone for every real configuration](figures/heat_flow_energy/rodgers_tao_sundman_clock.png)', '', '*The Rodgers–Tao energy is an exact Sundman clock with speed limit 4, but monotone for every real configuration; the near-collision law separates Davenport–Heilbronn from ζ.* [Theorem + Numerical · 4.4]', '',
     '![A Hardy constant for the primes is window positivity restated](figures/hardy_constant/hardy_constant_primes.png)', '', '*A Hardy constant for the primes is window positivity restated: ζ’s margin closes (critical), Davenport–Heilbronn crosses 1 near ℓ = 4–5, and local constants do not glue.* [Theorem + Numerical · 3.9]', '',
     '![Sundman/Levi-Civita clock](figures/sundman_clock/sundman_levi_civita_clock.png)', '', '*Sundman/Levi-Civita clock: the F/V collision becomes a turning point and real τ bounces; positivity is the clock staying real, i.e. RH restated.* [Theorem · 4.7 (circular as a proof route, Restatement 4.4)]', '',
     '![Two Kepler degenerations](figures/sundman_clock/two_kepler_degenerations.png)', '', '*Two Kepler degenerations: F = V is the circular orbit (e = 0), not the Levi-Civita centre collision (e = 1); both share the square-root map.* [Theorem · 4.4]', '',
